{
  "x-version": 1,
  "x-status": "designed",
  "description": "Designed (docs/native/capture-split.md 4.3 \"Idempotency, on the hub\", 11 H1b to H1k, #773), not implemented. The hub's rules for `POST /api/station/urgent` as a table a test can run: each scenario is a list of steps on the hub's clock and what the hub must answer and hold afterwards. tests/contracts_capture.py `Hub` is the reference model that passes it; the hub's route, and the stub hub the station's tests use, must give the same answers. A step has `at` (seconds on the hub's clock since the scenario began; the on-air event of `alarm` is at 0) and one of: `urgent` (a request's items: each names an entry of `items`, with keys to replace; `data` is merged; `station` defaults to st-north) with `expect` (the status per item, in order; or `expect_http` for a request refused whole, with `reason`; `off_contract`: a body no conforming sender makes, which the hub must survive); `event` (one event of the app's lane, /api/station/events: its times are on the hub's clock here); `ack` (a person acknowledges the alarm that hangs on this traffic id); `state_paging` (the `paging` list of the app's state post); `hub` (`handoff`: stop leading; `lead`: lead again, with a new generation; `{restore_s: n}`: crash and come back from a replica n seconds old). `then` is what holds at the end: how many alarms exist, how many pushes went out, how many late notices; `traffic_ids`, what each alarm hangs on; `page`, the live page under its key. `params.max_age_s` is A7, NOT DECIDED by the admin: 1800 is the recommended rule, and a scenario that depends on it says so (`depends_on`).",
